# Chroma library, modern Earth: CAUGHT BETWEEN (S3 "Their places' eyes", v22.3, cast_want: caught).
# Two of the character's places read the same act differently; a friend who goes to both brings it up.
# 20 moments, 10 pairs (WB WR UR UG BG and their partners). Slots {place_a} (led by the pair's first colour), {place_b}.
# Every option carries caught: side_a | side_b | bridge | apart | back. The Engine gates bridge on known in both places.
# Per way, its one-letter option plus its cross (first letter) = 140. Spec chroma-ideas/social-mechanics.md S3.


# Chroma library, modern Earth: CAUGHT BETWEEN TWO PLACES, set 1 (S3 "Their places' eyes", v22.3, DRAFT, cast_want: caught).
# Enemy pair WB: {place_a} is a duty-and-rules place (a congregation's committee, a union, a residents' council, the
# volunteer watch), {place_b} an ambition-and-advantage place (a business club, a traders' association, an ambitious
# firm's team, a networking circle). The Engine fills both names; every option's caught: is side_a, side_b, bridge,
# apart or back. Syntax: header of build.py. Each colour's one-colour chance plus its cross chance sums to 140.
#   pair 1  one act read two ways             1a WB (s1) / 1b URG (s2)
#   pair 2  two places ask opposite things     2a WB (s3) / 2b URG (s4)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: one act read two ways

== the step one place cheers and the other frowns on
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WB
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, friends
horizon: months
roles: regular, go-between, keeper
who: go-between
cast_want: caught
earth: two of your places pull apart over a step you took lately: one cheers it as bold, the other frowns on it as selfish
times: People whose circles judge the same act differently tend to decide later and take part less in both (cross-pressures: Lazarsfeld 1944; Mutz 2002)
gap: 3-8
scene: Word of the step {N} took this spring has gone round. At {place_b} people shake {Ns} hand and call it bold; at {place_a} the greetings have gone cool, and someone has said {N} put themselves first. Now both want {N} to answer for it, and a friend who goes to both passes on what each side is saying.
scene W: The people at {place_a} have stood by {N} for years. Fairness says they are owed an honest account, face to face.
scene U: Both places are reading one step by different rules. {N} wants to understand each reading before saying a word.
scene B: The doors are opening at {place_b} now. A cool welcome at {place_a} is a price, and {N} can weigh a price.
scene R: {N} is proud of the step and stung by the coolness. Being judged for it makes {Ns} face burn.
scene G: Both places are part of who {N} is. Neither tie should be cut over one season of talk.
- W | stand with {place_a}: give a full, honest account there, and make amends | caught: side_a | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 80
- U | stand with {place_b}, and set out plainly why the step made sense | caught: side_b | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 60
- B | bring {place_a} and {place_b} to one table, and show each what it gains | caught: bridge | v: power, achievement | chance: 55
- R | enjoy the praise at {place_b}, and never mention the step at {place_a} | caught: apart | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 80
- G | stay home among family for a season, and let the talk die down | caught: back | v: tradition, security | chance: 85
- W>U | ask both places for a fair hearing, so each learns the other's reasons | caught: bridge | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 60
- U>B | plan what to say at each place, so neither costs the other anything | caught: apart | v: achievement, security | chance: 80
- B>R | stop going to either for a season; no place gets to judge the step | caught: back | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 85
- R>G | go to {place_a} tonight, admit the coolness hurt, and ask to be let back in | caught: side_a | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 60
- G>W | stand by {place_b}, who backed the step, as loyal people do | caught: side_b | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 55
works: The talk settles, and one of the two places greets {N} more warmly than before.
works: By the end of the season both places have heard {Ns} side, and the step is old news at each.
fails: One of the places cools for good, and {N} feels it every time the door opens there.
fails: Both places go a little cool, and the story comes back to {N} told wrong.

== the choice one place honours and the other resents
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: URG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, friends
horizon: months
roles: regular, go-between, colleague
who: go-between
cast_want: caught
earth: two of your places pull apart over a choice you made: one honours it as the decent thing, the other resents what it cost
times: Different circles judge one act by different standards, and a person between them is pulled both ways (Graham, Haidt and Nosek 2009; role strain: Goode 1960)
gap: 3-8
scene: The choice {N} made last month has reached both places. At {place_a} people say {N} did the decent thing, and they are proud of it; at {place_b} it is called naive and costly, and one or two have stopped returning calls. Each place now wants {N} to answer for it: one wants {N} to own it out loud, the other wants to hear it will not happen again.
scene W: {N} did what was right and would do it again. The only question is how to stand by it without wrecking things at {place_b}.
scene U: {N} sees two honest readings of one act: one counts what was owed, the other counts what it cost. Both have a point.
scene B: The praise at {place_a} does not open many doors. The doors at {place_b} do, and they are closing a little.
scene R: {N} is fed up with being judged. It felt right at the time, and it still does.
scene G: {N} was brought up to do things this way. That is not something to apologise for, to anyone.
- W | stand with {place_b}: keep to its rules from here on, as a member should | caught: side_b | v: conformity, security | chance: 60
- U | lay out the facts at both places, so each sees why the other reads it so | caught: bridge | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 55
- B | let {place_a} honour it, and tell {place_b} it was a one-off | caught: apart | v: power, security | chance: 78
- R | stay away from both for a while, sick of being judged | caught: back | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 88
- G | stand with {place_a}, among the people who taught this way of doing things | caught: side_a | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 85
- W>B | keep a firm rule: neither place's business is discussed at the other | caught: apart | v: security, conformity | chance: 80
- U>R | plan a season off from both, and use it to think freely | caught: back | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B>G | use the praise at {place_a} to win a firmer place there, and let {place_b} go | caught: side_a | v: achievement, tradition | chance: 62
- R>W | go to {place_b} tonight, admit what it cost them, and offer to make it right | caught: side_b | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 52
- G>U | invite an old friend from each place home, and let them talk it through slowly | caught: bridge | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 55
works: One place takes {N} closer than ever, and the other comes round in its own time.
works: The two readings meet halfway, and people at both places start to ask {N} what the other side thinks.
fails: The calls from one place stop for good, and {N} misses more than expected.
fails: Neither place is quite satisfied, and {N} is talked about at both for the rest of the year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: two places ask opposite things in one season

== a call to stand up, a call to keep the peace
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WB
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, friends
horizon: months
roles: regular, go-between, neighbour
who: go-between
cast_want: caught
earth: two of your places pull apart over a cause: one wants you to stand up and be counted, the other wants you to keep the peace
times: When a person's circles disagree on a cause, they are slower to make up their mind and likelier to stay out of it (cross-pressures: Lazarsfeld 1944; Mutz 2002)
gap: 3-8
scene: It is a hard season in the neighbourhood. At {place_a} they are asking everyone to stand up and be counted: sign the letter, speak at the meeting, be seen. At {place_b} the word is the opposite: keep out of it, it is bad for business, and some members sit on the other side. A friend who goes to both warns {N} that both places will notice which way {N} goes.
scene W: When a cause is just, standing up is owed. But {N} has made promises at {place_b} too.
scene U: {N} wants to know the cause well before signing anything: what it asks, who it hurts, what it could change.
scene B: Standing up costs something at {place_b}, where the deals are made. Staying quiet costs something at {place_a}. {N} counts both.
scene R: Part of {N} wants to be at the front with a sign today, and let {place_b} think what it likes.
scene G: {N} has known people in both places for years. One season's quarrel should not break what took years to grow.
- W | call a fair meeting where both places put their case, and chair it | caught: bridge | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 55
- U | back the cause quietly in writing at {place_a}, and say nothing at {place_b} | caught: apart | v: self-direction, security | chance: 80
- B | sit this season out at both, and keep every door open | caught: back | v: power, security | chance: 82
- R | stand with {place_a} at the front, and say out loud why | caught: side_a | v: stimulation, universalism | chance: 88
- G | stand with {place_b} and keep the peace, for the sake of old friends there | caught: side_b | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 62
- W>R | decline both politely, as any member may, and keep the season free | caught: back | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 85
- U>G | study the cause, then stand with {place_a} for what it would keep alive | caught: side_a | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 60
- B>W | stand with {place_b}, and get its promise to help the cause another way | caught: side_b | v: power, universalism | chance: 58
- R>U | take a friend from each place out tonight, and argue it through till they understand | caught: bridge | v: stimulation, universalism | chance: 52
- G>B | keep the two in separate corners of life, as always, and lose neither | caught: apart | v: security, tradition | chance: 78
works: The season turns, and one place remembers warmly where {N} stood when it counted.
works: A few people from both places end up talking for the first time, and {N} is the one they thank.
fails: One place takes it badly, and {N} is left off the next round of invitations there.
fails: Both places sense {N} held back, and each trusts {N} a little less.

== two places want the same evenings
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: URG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: regular, go-between, keeper
who: go-between
cast_want: caught
earth: two of your places pull apart over your evenings: one has a big push on, the other is counting on your loyalty this season
times: Two roles that claim the same hours are a common strain, met by choosing one, keeping them apart or stepping back from both (role strain: Goode 1960)
gap: 3-8
scene: Both places want the same few months of {Ns} evenings. At {place_b} there is a big push on, and those who put in the hours now will be remembered when the rewards are shared. At {place_a} they are short of hands this season and are counting on {N}, who has always pulled their weight. Neither knows yet that the other has asked.
scene W: {N} said yes to {place_a} long ago, and a promise does not lapse because something brighter comes along.
scene U: There are only so many evenings. {N} lays out the weeks and asks what each place really needs, and when.
scene B: The push at {place_b} could make {Ns} name. The work at {place_a} will still be there next year.
scene R: {N} can feel the buzz of the push and the weight of the need, and wants to answer both at once.
scene G: The people at {place_a} have been {Ns} people for a long time. That counts for something, whatever {place_b} offers.
- W | keep strict hours: fixed nights for each place, and no talk of one at the other | caught: apart | v: conformity, security | chance: 76
- U | say no to both this season; spread this thin, nothing would be done well | caught: back | v: self-direction, security | chance: 80
- B | stand with {place_a}: a name for reliability there pays off in the long run | caught: side_a | v: achievement, power | chance: 82
- R | throw in with the push at {place_b}; that is where the life is this season | caught: side_b | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 60
- G | bring people from {place_b} to lend a hand at {place_a}, as neighbours once did | caught: bridge | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 50
- W>G | keep the old promise to {place_a}, and stay with the people counting on it | caught: side_a | v: conformity, tradition | mark: kept your word | chance: 64
- U>W | weigh it up, and give the season to {place_b}, where the work helps more people | caught: side_b | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 60
- B>U | broker a deal: {place_b} lends hands to {place_a}, and each learns how the other works | caught: bridge | v: power, universalism | chance: 58
- R>B | say yes to both on the spot, and let each think it comes first | caught: apart | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 80
- G>R | let the season pass at home with family, free of both | caught: back | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 90
works: One place gets the best of {N} this season, and says so warmly at the end of it.
works: The two places find they can share {N} after all, and {N} becomes the one who links them.
fails: The place that lost out says nothing, but the welcome there is cooler for a long while.
fails: Stretched between the two, {N} lets both down a little, and both notice.

